WEBVTT

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:08.976
This dam held a mine's waste: rock
ground to powder, mixed with water.

00:00:09.326 --> 00:00:13.413
At about one in the morning, in
August twenty fourteen, it let go.

00:00:13.763 --> 00:00:16.131
This was Mount Polley,
in British Columbia.

00:00:16.481 --> 00:00:19.981
About twenty-five million cubic
metres of water and tailings

00:00:19.981 --> 00:00:22.983
poured out, into a creek
and the lakes below it.

00:00:23.333 --> 00:00:27.745
From space, the creek went from
a thread to a wide grey scar.

00:00:28.095 --> 00:00:29.441
No one was killed.

00:00:29.791 --> 00:00:32.857
But a dam like this is
built to stand for good.

00:00:33.207 --> 00:00:34.414
What gave way?

00:00:35.664 --> 00:00:40.169
The answer most people reach
for is the wall: it burst.

00:00:40.519 --> 00:00:43.677
An independent panel of experts
looked hard at the wall.

00:00:44.027 --> 00:00:47.867
It found flaws: a core cut
thinner than first planned,

00:00:47.867 --> 00:00:50.575
and samples of filter sand
that were too coarse.

00:00:50.925 --> 00:00:53.247
But none of them caused the breach.

00:00:53.597 --> 00:00:57.417
It found no sign that the pond
had poured over the top first,

00:00:57.417 --> 00:01:00.702
or that water had washed
the dam out from inside.

00:01:01.052 --> 00:01:04.070
And what was left didn't
look like a burst.

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:09.018
The crest had dropped more than
three metres, along one clean break.

00:01:09.368 --> 00:01:12.712
The rockfill's layers were
tilted, but still in order.

00:01:13.062 --> 00:01:16.173
The whole mass had moved
eleven metres outward.

00:01:16.523 --> 00:01:21.028
And beyond the toe, the ground itself
had been shoved up, about three metres.

00:01:21.378 --> 00:01:25.093
A wall that bursts doesn't
lift the ground beyond it.

00:01:25.443 --> 00:01:27.347
Something underneath had slid.

00:01:27.697 --> 00:01:30.297
The crest dropped further
than the water stood below

00:01:30.297 --> 00:01:33.781
it, so the pond spilled
over, and cut the breach.

00:01:35.031 --> 00:01:37.671
Eight to ten metres under
the old ground surface,

00:01:37.671 --> 00:01:40.325
there was a thin layer of silt and clay.

00:01:40.675 --> 00:01:44.762
It was laid down in a lake at
the end of the last ice age.

00:01:45.112 --> 00:01:48.223
The ground had been drilled,
to see what was down there.

00:01:48.573 --> 00:01:53.914
But only four holes with tests went that
deep, and none of them where the dam broke.

00:01:54.264 --> 00:01:56.586
The layer was missed where it mattered.

00:01:56.936 --> 00:02:00.326
Picture the castle at the
beach, standing on firm sand.

00:02:00.676 --> 00:02:05.227
A hand's depth below it, there's
a thin stripe of wet grey sand.

00:02:05.577 --> 00:02:08.549
Push a stick in here, and here, and here.

00:02:08.899 --> 00:02:11.685
Every time, you stop short of the stripe.

00:02:12.035 --> 00:02:16.540
So how much can a thin seam matter,
under a dam forty metres tall?

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:20.391
The course has a sandbox for exactly this.

00:02:20.741 --> 00:02:23.527
It's a teaching model,
not the panel's analysis.

00:02:23.877 --> 00:02:27.557
Here's the series' dam: a clay
core, with rockfill on either

00:02:27.557 --> 00:02:31.029
side, on clay with a weak
layer four metres down.

00:02:31.379 --> 00:02:34.583
That's the same share of
its height as the real one.

00:02:34.933 --> 00:02:38.473
Engineers judge a slope by
its factor of safety: the

00:02:38.473 --> 00:02:41.806
grip the soil has, divided
by the grip it needs.

00:02:42.156 --> 00:02:44.200
Below one, it slides.

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:45.804
First, the water.

00:02:46.154 --> 00:02:49.254
The seepage solver says
this dam barely leaks:

00:02:49.254 --> 00:02:52.191
about two buckets a day,
for each metre of dam.

00:02:52.541 --> 00:02:56.488
And the sand at its toe is safe
from boiling, with room to spare.

00:02:56.838 --> 00:02:57.906
Then the slope.

00:02:58.256 --> 00:03:00.676
The course's check tries
more than a thousand

00:03:00.676 --> 00:03:02.993
circles through the dam and the ground.

00:03:03.343 --> 00:03:05.883
For each one, it weighs the grip along the

00:03:05.883 --> 00:03:08.173
circle against the weight trying to slide.

00:03:08.523 --> 00:03:11.356
The weakest circle gives
one point three one.

00:03:11.706 --> 00:03:14.910
But a circle is only the
shape the check expects.

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:18.436
A slide doesn't have to be round.

00:03:18.786 --> 00:03:22.454
The seam offers a flat path,
so try a block instead.

00:03:22.804 --> 00:03:27.402
It drops at the crest, slides along
the seam, and comes up beyond the toe.

00:03:27.752 --> 00:03:30.632
A second method, made for
shapes like this, finds

00:03:30.632 --> 00:03:33.557
one point two one: weaker than any circle.

00:03:33.907 --> 00:03:39.155
And look at its shape: the crest drops,
and the far end rises beyond the toe.

00:03:39.505 --> 00:03:40.991
That's the real slide.

00:03:41.341 --> 00:03:43.570
Now take the seam out of the ground.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:47.560
The same dam gives one
point five five, and passes

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.911
the course's target of one point five.

00:03:50.261 --> 00:03:52.350
That's the ground the design pictured.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:55.626
But one point two one is still above one.

00:03:55.976 --> 00:03:58.066
Something had to push it over.

00:03:59.316 --> 00:04:00.802
That was the slope.

00:04:01.152 --> 00:04:04.496
The design called for two
across for every one up.

00:04:04.846 --> 00:04:07.226
But for years, the
outer face had been left

00:04:07.226 --> 00:04:10.418
at one point three
across for every one up.

00:04:10.768 --> 00:04:15.041
That's rockfill tipped from trucks and
left at the steepest it will stand.

00:04:15.391 --> 00:04:18.874
It's steeper than a cone
of dry sand at the beach.

00:04:19.224 --> 00:04:22.475
And rock was still being placed
there the week it failed.

00:04:22.825 --> 00:04:26.633
The sandbox can't go that steep,
but try its steepest slope.

00:04:26.983 --> 00:04:30.187
Without the seam, it
gives one point two eight.

00:04:30.537 --> 00:04:34.020
With the seam, one point oh one.

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:36.181
Each one alone leaves a margin.

00:04:36.531 --> 00:04:38.389
Together, they leave almost none.

00:04:38.739 --> 00:04:43.615
In the panel's words, building on the clay
without knowing it was loading the gun.

00:04:43.965 --> 00:04:46.148
And the steep slope pulled the trigger.

00:04:47.398 --> 00:04:50.323
But the seam had held for seventeen years.

00:04:50.673 --> 00:04:52.856
Standing that long didn't prove it safe.

00:04:53.206 --> 00:04:57.006
Each raise took the dam closer
to the edge, and by that summer,

00:04:57.006 --> 00:05:00.497
the panel found, its factor
of safety was about one.

00:05:00.847 --> 00:05:03.167
A few hours before it
went, the night shift's

00:05:03.167 --> 00:05:06.513
log read: good slope, no visible cracks.

00:05:06.863 --> 00:05:10.299
So why did it go in one
night, with no warning?

00:05:10.649 --> 00:05:13.111
Back to the idea behind this whole series.

00:05:13.461 --> 00:05:16.665
Soil's grip comes from its
grains pressing on each other.

00:05:17.015 --> 00:05:21.148
Water pressure in the gaps pushes them
apart, and takes that press away.

00:05:21.498 --> 00:05:25.074
Stand still on the wet sand at
the waterline, and it holds you.

00:05:25.424 --> 00:05:27.421
Wiggle your feet, and you sink.

00:05:27.771 --> 00:05:31.161
Same sand: what changed
is how it was pushed.

00:05:32.411 --> 00:05:36.916
Seventeen years of raising had pressed
the clay harder than anything in its past.

00:05:37.266 --> 00:05:41.027
Clay in that state, when it's
sheared, tries to pack tighter.

00:05:41.377 --> 00:05:46.486
Pushed slowly, its water has time to seep
out, and the grains keep their press.

00:05:46.836 --> 00:05:50.783
Clay's gaps are tiny, so water
creeps through it very slowly.

00:05:51.133 --> 00:05:54.198
Over seventeen years of
raising, there was time.

00:05:54.548 --> 00:05:57.056
In the minutes of a slide, there isn't.

00:05:57.406 --> 00:06:01.353
The water is trapped, its pressure
rises, and the grip falls.

00:06:01.703 --> 00:06:05.186
The water pressure came from the
shearing, not from the weight.

00:06:05.536 --> 00:06:08.056
The panel's tests found
that, sheared fast,

00:06:08.056 --> 00:06:10.459
the clay had less than half its slow grip.

00:06:10.809 --> 00:06:12.992
And as it moved, it lost more.

00:06:13.342 --> 00:06:15.849
The design was checked
against the slow grip.

00:06:16.199 --> 00:06:18.754
But a slide that starts is fast.

00:06:21.104 --> 00:06:22.590
The dam didn't break.

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:27.212
The ground under it lost its
grip, the moment it began to move.

00:06:29.562 --> 00:06:31.234
So what would have held it?

00:06:31.584 --> 00:06:35.949
Back in the sandbox, try the
fixes from the episode on leaks.

00:06:36.299 --> 00:06:38.203
Drains saved that dam.

00:06:38.553 --> 00:06:42.193
Here, the factor barely
moves: the seam lies sealed

00:06:42.193 --> 00:06:45.473
in clay, below the dam,
where drains can't reach.

00:06:45.823 --> 00:06:48.843
A wall down through the
ground stops nearly all the

00:06:48.843 --> 00:06:51.907
leak, but the block still
falls short of the target.

00:06:52.257 --> 00:06:54.532
What works is a flatter slope.

00:06:54.882 --> 00:06:57.622
Two and a half across is
still short of the target.

00:06:57.972 --> 00:07:01.176
At three across for
every one up, it passes.

00:07:01.526 --> 00:07:04.777
The extra weight beyond the
toe holds the block back.

00:07:05.127 --> 00:07:08.467
The course calls a gentler
slope, or a heavy berm

00:07:08.467 --> 00:07:11.768
at the toe, the usual fix
for a weak foundation.

00:07:12.118 --> 00:07:15.378
At the real dam, the panel found
that flattening the slope to

00:07:15.378 --> 00:07:18.945
two across, as first designed,
would have avoided the failure.

00:07:20.195 --> 00:07:22.331
So why did so much come out?

00:07:22.681 --> 00:07:27.232
At the breach, there was no beach of
tailings between the pond and the dam.

00:07:27.582 --> 00:07:29.579
The water stood right against it.

00:07:29.929 --> 00:07:33.876
The panel called that high water
the last link in the chain.

00:07:34.226 --> 00:07:37.406
With the water a metre
lower and a wider beach, it

00:07:37.406 --> 00:07:40.682
wrote, that last link
might have held until dawn.

00:07:41.032 --> 00:07:44.654
Instead, the water carried out
about a third of the tailings.

00:07:45.004 --> 00:07:49.880
Then the panel set out what to aim for, from
the first principles of soil mechanics.

00:07:50.230 --> 00:07:52.273
Keep the water off the top.

00:07:52.623 --> 00:07:54.109
Keep the tailings drained.

00:07:54.459 --> 00:07:55.992
And pack them dense.

00:07:56.342 --> 00:07:58.664
That's this whole series, in one list.

00:07:59.014 --> 00:08:02.254
It also asked for a harder look
at the ground before building,

00:08:02.254 --> 00:08:05.330
and for independent boards
to review tailings dams.

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:08.660
Its warning: if nothing
changed, British Columbia

00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:11.485
could expect two failures every ten years.

00:08:11.835 --> 00:08:15.132
In twenty sixteen, the province
rewrote its rules for them.

00:08:16.382 --> 00:08:18.333
Seven dams, one idea.

00:08:18.683 --> 00:08:24.043
A beach, a concrete dam, a
clay core, a leak, a boil,

00:08:24.043 --> 00:08:27.227
a beach of mine waste,
and a seam nobody found.

00:08:27.577 --> 00:08:31.293
Every time, the soil held while
its grains pressed on each other.

00:08:31.643 --> 00:08:34.893
And every time, water in the
gaps could take that away.

00:08:35.243 --> 00:08:37.101
Dry sand pours.

00:08:37.451 --> 00:08:38.937
Damp sand stands.

00:08:39.287 --> 00:08:42.399
And soaked sand, pushed, turns to soup.
